{
  // The whole game: every type of decompile\Terraria and decompile\ReLogic, translated. Paths relative to this file.
  // Run: sh run.sh (writes port\crates\terraria-gen-game\src\gen, src\host\api, GAME_REPORT.md, HOST_API.md)
  "include": ["native.json"],
  // float expressions as the game's x86 JIT evaluates them (DESIGN.md section 5): x87 precision, and the float
  // locals the JIT keeps on the x87 stack are not rounded at a store (port/crates/terraria-x87/FLOAT_RULE.md)
  "floatMode": "jit32",
  // Float locals the real JIT keeps in memory although the allocation rule (computed on the decompiler's locals)
  // puts them on the x87 stack. Each entry names the comparison with the real game that shows it.
  "floatInMemory": {
    "Terraria.Collision.CheckLinevLine:num": "machine code of the collision reference run (work/jit/collref, RefDumpCollisionJit.exe, refdata/collision reproduced byte for byte): `fstp dword [ebp-4]` right after num is computed, every later read `fld dword [ebp-4]` (the JIT's common subexpressions a2-a1, b2-b1 hold the registers). With num rounded and num4 spilled (floatSpillAtAlloc) the 20,000 calls of refdata/collision/CheckLinevLine.bin are exact (859 differ without)",
    "Terraria.Graphics.Light.TileLightScanner.ApplySurfaceLight:num6": "machine code of the lighting reference run (work/jit/lightref, RefDumpLightJit.exe, refdata/light reproduced byte for byte): num4, num5 and num6 are each stored with `fstp dword` ([ebp-0x10], [ebp-0x14], [ebp-0x18]) right after the division by 255 and read back from there (num7 = `fld [ebp-0x10]; fadd [ebp-0x14]; fadd [ebp-0x18]`, num = num4 ... `fld [ebp-0x18]`); the rule keeps num6 alone on the x87 stack",
    "Terraria.Graphics.Light.TileLightScanner.ApplyHellLight:num7": "machine code of the lighting reference run (work/jit/lightref): `num7 = 0.7f - num6` with num6 = 0.2f is folded by the JIT to the float constant 0.5 (`fadd dword [const] = 0.5` in the DiscoR/G/B case); unrounded it is 0.49999998509883881, rounded to float (in memory) it is 0.5",
    "Terraria.Main.UpdateWeather:num7": "refdata/ticks world scenario: Main.windSpeedCurrent after tick 1560 is 0.20025914907455444 in the real server; that value needs num7 (`num7 += Math.Abs(num8 - windSpeedCurrent) * 0.0015f`) rounded to float at its store (60 steps from the tick-1500 state modelled both ways: rounded 0.20025914907455444, unrounded 0.20025913417339325)"
  },
  // Float locals the real JIT keeps on the x87 stack across the calls they are live across, spilled to an 8-byte
  // slot (`fstp qword` / `fld qword`), so never rounded, although the allocation rule puts them in memory. Each entry
  // names the machine code that shows it (FloatAlloc / Emit: on the stack, left out of the rounding at calls). Empty:
  // its first entry (AI_065_Butterflies:num22) is an evaluation-stack value, which jitFloatAlloc now finds by itself.
  "floatStackAcrossCalls": {
  },
  // IL sizes of the original methods (gen_il_sizes.py, from db/terraria.sqlite): which methods the JIT does not optimise
  "ilSizes": "il_sizes.tsv",
  // what the real JIT inlined / called at each mscorlib call site of each float method (gen_jit_calls.py: the reference
  // runs' own code where a reference run ran the method, the whole-game jitdump work/jit/all elsewhere)
  "jitCalls": "jit_calls.tsv",
  // what the JIT of the reference runs inlined / called in the methods that ran there (gen_jit_refcalls.py from
  // work/jit/ref/<scenario>: tools/refdump_worldgen/run_refjit.sh repeats p1, framing, collision, lighting, sky, liquid,
  // ticks and the six worldgen runs, each reproducing its refdata byte for byte, then dumps the code of every method
  // that ran in that process; game callees included; a method with different answers in two runs is left out and
  // listed in jit_refcalls_conflicts.tsv). Decides where x87 values are rounded for those pairs, before jitCalls and
  // the size rule (FloatAlloc.CallKind)
  "jitRefCalls": "jit_refcalls.tsv",
  // the sites of the pairs above that are a call instruction at some sites only (gen_jit_refcalls.py, through ILSpy's
  // PDB of the decompilation: work/pdb/pdb_seq.tsv, `tmeta pdbseq`): decided site by site (FloatAlloc.SiteIsCall)
  "jitRefCallSites": "jit_refcall_sites.tsv",
  // where the real JIT kept each float local of the methods that ran in the reference runs (gen_jit_float_alloc.py:
  // the CLR's own variable locations of those processes, mapped to the decompiled names through ILSpy's PDB of the
  // same decompilation, tmeta pdblocals). Replaces the allocation rule there (FloatAlloc.cs); a float local that is
  // not an IL local is an evaluation-stack value: unrounded, kept across calls
  "jitFloatAlloc": "jit_float_alloc.tsv",
  // Float locals on the x87 stack that the original rounds at the array allocation they are live across (the
  // newarr helper is a call instruction). Only these locals, only in these methods.
  "floatSpillAtAlloc": {
    "Terraria.Collision.CheckLinevLine:num4": "machine code of the collision reference run (work/jit/collref): `fstp dword [ebp-8]` of num4 before `call` to the newarr helper of `new Vector2[1] { new Vector2(a1.X + num4 * ..., ...) }`, reloaded from there for the element; num5 is dead there. Evidence with floatInMemory CheckLinevLine:num"
  },
  // The x87 evaluation of an XNA member's body as its machine code in the reference run does it, used at the call
  // sites of jit_bodies.tsv (gen_jit_bodies.py; the dumps of all reference runs, work/jit/ref/<scenario>, as for
  // jitRefCalls) where those runs compiled a call instruction to it at every IL site. The nativeMembers template (f32 arithmetic) stays
  // everywhere else.
  //   Vector2.Length():           fld X; fmul X; fld Y; fmul Y; faddp; fstp qword; fld qword; fsqrt; fstp dword
  //   Vector2.Normalize(Vector2): the same sum and sqrt rounded to float, 1 / it kept unrounded (fld1; fdivrp), then
  //                               X * it and Y * it each rounded at the store
  //   Vector2.Lerp, Vector3.Lerp, Vector2.SmoothStep, MathHelper.Lerp / SmoothStep, Vector2 / float (op_Division:
  //                               1 / d unrounded), Distance (differences unrounded), DistanceSquared, Dot, LengthSquared,
  //                               the instance Normalize(), CatmullRom (one rounding per component), Reflect (the dot product unrounded): read from the entity reference runs (work/jit/ref/entities_*,
  //                               tools/refdump_worldgen/jit_disasm.py). A value prefixed "f64:" is a body whose float
  //                               result is returned in ST0 without a store: unrounded where the caller keeps computing
  //                               (Emit.F64), rounded where it stores it
  //   Vector4.Lerp(v1, v2, t):    per component fld v2.X; fsub v1.X; fmul t; fadd v1.X; fstp dword (the lighting
  //                               reference run, work/jit/lightref: one rounding per component, at the store)
  // Evidence: CheckAABBvLineCollision6 (20,000 calls) is reproduced exactly by this Length and differs in 503 calls
  // with the f32 one (model of the dumped machine code against refdata/collision; then the sim gate).
  // LiquidRenderer.GetShimmerBaseColor: the x87 Lerp reproduces all 888 points of refdata/light/shimmer.bin, the f32
  // one differs on 104 (model with terraria-x87's fsin); then the sim gate (scramble_shimmer 458 tiles -> 0).
  //   Vector2.Transform(v, m), Vector3.Transform(v, m), Matrix * Matrix: per component the products summed on the x87
  //                               stack (fld; fmul; fld; fmul; faddp; fadd), one rounding at the store; Matrix.CreateRotationZ:
  //                               fcos / fsin (the processor's own, FLOAT_RULE.md 10.17) each stored as a float, M21 by fchs
  //                               (the coverage run, work/jit/client/cover: DrawPlayer_TransformDrawData, a UFO mount's
  //                               sprite 1 ulp off in y; FLOAT_RULE.md section 10, item 27); the by-reference
  //                               Vector2.Transform(ref, ref, out) the same (inlined in DynamicSpriteFont.InternalDraw,
  //                               work/jit/client/probe: a rotated crit number's glyph 1 ulp off in x, item 32)
  //   Vector3.Normalize():        as Vector2's instance Normalize, three squares (ReflectiveArmorShaderData.Apply's light
  //                               direction 1 ulp off in y; work/jit/client/probe, FLOAT_RULE.md section 10, item 35)
